I don't agree with the boolean idea of wether or not the monster is behind
another.  Simply because if there was a killer bee in front..  It could
not block a umber hulk, however, it could easily block another bee, or a xan.

I propose, that you use a numbering system.  A killer bee or xan would
have a number of one.  and for example an umber hulk a 10.
now, if a bee was directly behind the umber hulk.. you could never see it.
 However, A bee could block out an umber hulk 10 spaces away, due to the
fact that objects appear smaller when far away.  Therefore..  You would
need a simple algorithm, such as:

1st monster # + distance - 2nd monster #.
then you would just look at the value,  if the number is 0 or below, the
monster is invisible.  1 or more, and you can see it.

I think that would be MUCH more realistic.



